cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Unallocated: what tokenizer vocabularies were spent on, and what they weren't"
message: >-
  If you use this study, its data, or its measurements, please cite it as below,
  and cite the underlying corpora separately (see NOTICE.md).
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: Weiss
    given-names: Leo
repository-code: "https://github.com/lwdata75/unallocated"
abstract: >-
  A measurement of tokenizer fertility across 204 languages and eight tokenizer
  families, using the sentence-aligned FLORES-200 and MASSIVE corpora. Token
  cost is computed per aligned sentence pair against English, then decomposed
  into a floor — the cost inherent to a writing system, taken as the best any
  measured tokenizer achieves — and neglect, the excess above that floor
  attributable to vocabulary that was never allocated to the language. The
  central result is that multilingual vocabulary allocation is scoped rather
  than general: BLOOM's median fertility across all 204 languages is marginally
  worse than a contemporary English-centric encoding's, while being dramatically
  better on the languages its training corpus covered. Provenance for every
  input is machine-recorded and verified on each run.
keywords:
  - tokenization
  - multilingual NLP
  - language equity
  - FLORES-200
  - MASSIVE
  - subword vocabularies
license: MIT
